summary:
- topic: FY2026 Budget Overview
  text: The Superintendent presented the proposed $73 million FY2026 budget, representing
    a 5.98% increase and requiring an additional $3.18 million from taxpayers. A significant
    variable is health insurance, budgeted at a 5% increase, while the actual statewide
    range could be between 3% and 14%.
- topic: Board Vacancy Announcement
  text: The Board Chair announced the resignation of at-large board member Jen Ryan.
    The City Council will oversee the process to fill the vacancy.
- topic: Leadership Priorities and 'Unfinished Work'
  text: Superintendent Metheny strongly defended retaining the Director of Mental
    and Behavioral Health and the Director of Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (DEI),
    presenting data on student mental health crises and significant academic achievement
    gaps between white students and students of color as evidence of critical, unfinished
    work.
- topic: Budget Restorations
  text: The superintendent's proposed budget restores a halftime STEM coordinator,
    one ESOL teacher, and the percussion ed tech position. The potential restoration
    of an ESOL EdTech position is contingent on receiving federal Title III funds.
- topic: Board Deliberations on Cuts
  text: During deliberations, a strong consensus emerged among board members to restore
    the three Social-Emotional Learning (SEL) teacher positions, citing overwhelming
    positive feedback from parents and staff on their impact on elementary students.
- topic: Funding Proposals and Salary Freeze
  text: To fund restorations, some board members proposed eliminating the Director
    of Mental and Behavioral Health position. A more contentious proposal involved
    freezing the salaries for the 12 highest-paid, non-union central office administrators,
    which garnered support from several board members as a measure of shared sacrifice.
- topic: Administrator Counter-Offer
  text: In response to the salary freeze discussion, Principal Rebecca Stern, speaking
    for school administrators, offered to find the equivalent savings ($67,000) from
    their collective school operational budgets. This offer was met with discomfort
    by some board members, who viewed it as an inappropriate bargaining chip.
- topic: No Final Decision Reached
  text: The board was unable to reach a consensus on any amendments to the budget.
    Faced with deep divisions over the proposed salary freeze and the introduction
    of new information, the board failed to bring a motion to a vote and adjourned
    with plans to schedule another meeting for further deliberation.
